TargetCheck local guard: reject a zero-start range ending at WGINT_MAX

Status: LOCAL ADDITION PENDING UPSTREAM REVIEW. This is not an upstream
GNU Wget commit and has not been reviewed or approved upstream. It is
applied only to the "complete" variant, after upstream commits
43d3ba9336bc94937e6fae2365c6ffd30c34ffcf and
01ff771caac1958662ca8665eed2021ec386a7af.

After a successful parse, gethttp() in src/http.c computes

    contlen = last_byte_pos - first_byte_pos + 1;

With an unknown entity length ("*"), the upstream-fixed parser accepts
"bytes 0-9223372036854775807/*", and that expression overflows the
signed 64-bit wgint. Both positions are nonnegative and last >= first
after the existing checks, so the expression overflows only for a zero
first position with a WGINT_MAX last position. The guard rejects exactly
that range; "bytes 1-9223372036854775807/*" stays accepted. The
max-endpoint-caller-boundary case in
tests/content-range-cases.c covers both sides.

Residual, not addressed by this guard (found by code reading of the
pristine 1.21.4 src/http.c only; not exercised by any test here): after
the range check at line 4056, gethttp() also computes

    hs->contlen = contlen + contrange;

at line 4071 (the same sum is printed at lines 4083-4086), which equals
last_byte_pos + 1. For "bytes R-9223372036854775807/*" with R equal to a
nonzero resume offset (restval, from --start-pos=R or -c), the line 4056
check accepts the response, this guard does not apply because R > 0,
and that sum overflows wgint. As defined for this example, the guard and
the caller_length assertion cover only last_byte_pos - first_byte_pos + 1
with a zero first position. The complete variant is therefore not a
complete repair of caller-side overflow.

